Sample Spark Scala Program
// Import required Spark libraries
import org.apache.spark.sql.SparkSession

object WeatherAnalysis {
  def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {

    // 1. Create Spark Session
    val spark = SparkSession.builder()
      .appName("Weather Data Analysis")
      .master("local[*]")   // runs locally using all cores
      .getOrCreate()

    // 2. Read the dataset (CSV/text file)
    val df = spark.read
      .option("header", "true")     // first row is column names
      .option("inferSchema", "true") // automatically detect data types
      .csv("sample_weather.txt")

    // 3. Show the data
    df.show()

    // 4. Calculate averages
    df.createOrReplaceTempView("weather")

    val result = spark.sql("""
      SELECT 
        AVG(Temperature) AS Avg_Temperature,
        AVG(DewPoint) AS Avg_DewPoint,
        AVG(WindSpeed) AS Avg_WindSpeed
      FROM weather
    """)

    // 5. Display result
    result.show()

    // 6. Stop Spark session
    spark.stop()
  }
}


Step-by-Step Explanation
🔹 1. Import Library
import org.apache.spark.sql.SparkSession
Imports the core entry point of Spark
SparkSession is required to:
Read data
Run SQL queries
Work with DataFrames
🔹 2. Create Spark Session
val spark = SparkSession.builder()
  .appName("Weather Data Analysis")
  .master("local[*]")
  .getOrCreate()
What happens here:
.builder() → initializes Spark configuration
.appName() → gives a name to your application
.master("local[*]") → runs Spark locally using all CPU cores
.getOrCreate() → creates or reuses a session

👉 This is the starting point of any Spark program

🔹 3. Read the Dataset
val df = spark.read
  .option("header", "true")
  .option("inferSchema", "true")
  .csv("sample_weather.txt")
Explanation:
spark.read → used to load data
.csv() → reads CSV/text file
header = true → first row is column names
inferSchema = true → automatically detects:
Temperature → integer
WindSpeed → integer

👉 Data is stored in a DataFrame (df)

🔹 4. Display Data
df.show()
Prints the dataset in tabular format
Default → shows first 20 rows
🔹 5. Create Temporary SQL Table
df.createOrReplaceTempView("weather")
Converts DataFrame into a temporary SQL table
Allows you to run SQL queries on it
🔹 6. Calculate Averages using SQL
val result = spark.sql("""
  SELECT 
    AVG(Temperature) AS Avg_Temperature,
    AVG(DewPoint) AS Avg_DewPoint,
    AVG(WindSpeed) AS Avg_WindSpeed
  FROM weather
""")
Explanation:
spark.sql() → runs SQL query
AVG() → calculates average
AS → renames output columns

👉 This is similar to database queries (MySQL)

🔹 7. Show Result
result.show()
Displays the computed averages
🔹 8. Stop Spark
spark.stop()
Ends the Spark session
Frees resources
🧠 Key Concepts (Important for Viva)
Apache Spark → fast big data processing framework
DataFrame → table-like structure (rows & columns)
SparkSession → entry point of Spark
SQL in Spark → allows querying like a database
Distributed Processing → works on large datasets



HOW TO DO THIS ON JUPYTER?
Recommended: Jupyter Notebook + PySpark
👉 Tools involved:
Jupyter Notebook
Apache Spark
⚙️ Step-by-Step Setup
🔹 Step 1: Install Required Packages

Open terminal / command prompt:

pip install pyspark notebook
🔹 Step 2: Start Jupyter Notebook
jupyter notebook
Browser will open
Click New → Python 3 Notebook
🔹 Step 3: Write Spark Code in Notebook
🧾 Cell 1: Create Spark Session
from pyspark.sql import SparkSession

spark = SparkSession.builder \
    .appName("Weather Analysis") \
    .master("local[*]") \
    .getOrCreate()
🧾 Cell 2: Load Dataset
df = spark.read \
    .option("header", "true") \
    .option("inferSchema", "true") \
    .csv("sample_weather.txt")

df.show()
🧾 Cell 3: Calculate Averages
df.createOrReplaceTempView("weather")

result = spark.sql("""
SELECT 
  AVG(Temperature) AS Avg_Temperature,
  AVG(DewPoint) AS Avg_DewPoint,
  AVG(WindSpeed) AS Avg_WindSpeed
FROM weather
""")

result.show()
🧾 Cell 4: Stop Spark
spark.stop()
📁 Where to keep your dataset?
Put sample_weather.txt in the same folder as your notebook
OR
Give full path:
.csv("C:/Users/yourname/sample_weather.txt")
